From 4160e50e279ef61c14e3797167bcea37cce814f5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrew Youll <andrewyoull86@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 23:25:48 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 16/17] hevc arm: chroma (epel) motion compensation in NEON, 8
 bits

put_hevc_epel, _uni and _bi for the h, v and hv filters at every width
(2 to 64), bit-exact with the C; FFmpeg's 32-bit ARM NEON had luma
(qpel) only. The filters are all (-, +, +, -), so the first pass is
u8 x u8 multiply-accumulates with the outer taps subtracted (the sum
fits in 16 bits); hv's second pass is 16 x 16 -> 32 bits. Strips of 8
columns, the last one storing only 2, 4 or 6 where the width needs it.
Byte-element loads and lane stores, so nothing needs aligning (RISC OS
traps unaligned wider accesses). The whole-sample copies use the same
NEON functions as luma's (the C shares put_hevc_pel_pixels).
