index 0000000..5882c83
--- /dev/null
+++ b/libavcodec/arm/hevcdsp_epel_neon.S
@@ -0,0 +1,336 @@
+/*
+ * HEVC chroma (epel) motion compensation in NEON for 32-bit ARM, 8 bits
+ *
+ * WHAT IT DOES
+ *   HEVC predicts a block of chroma from a reference picture at a
+ *   position with 1/8-sample precision. The fractional part is
+ *   interpolated with one of seven 4-tap filters (ff_hevc_epel_filters),
+ *   horizontally (h), vertically (v) or both (hv, horizontal first).
+ *   The result goes out in one of three ways ("kinds" below):
+ *     0  int16_t, at 14-bit precision, into a MAX_PB_SIZE-wide buffer
+ *        (the first half of a bi-predicted block): put_hevc_epel_*
+ *     1  uint8_t pixels, rounded and clipped: put_hevc_epel_uni_*
+ *     2  uint8_t pixels from this prediction plus the int16 one in src2,
+ *        averaged, rounded and clipped: put_hevc_epel_bi_*
+ *   FFmpeg 5.1's 32-bit ARM NEON had luma (qpel) only; this is the chroma
+ *   half. Bit-exact with hevcdsp_template.c for every input:
+ *   tests/host/hevc_epel_test.c compares each function with the C (and is
+ *   shown to catch deliberate bugs), and run.sh decodes HEVC files with
+ *   and without NEON and on x86, picture by picture.
+ *
+ * THE C IT MIRRORS (hevcdsp_template.c, BIT_DEPTH 8)
+ *   F(src, stride) = f0*src[x-stride] + f1*src[x] + f2*src[x+stride] + f3*src[x+2*stride]
+ *   h, v:  kind 0: F                (">> (BIT_DEPTH - 8)" is ">> 0")
+ *          kind 1: clip_u8((F + 32) >> 6)
+ *          kind 2: clip_u8((F + src2[x] + 64) >> 7)
+ *   hv:    tmp = F_h over height + 3 rows, from the row above (int16)
+ *          A = F_v(tmp) >> 6, then kind 0: A; 1: clip_u8((A + 32) >> 6);
+ *          2: clip_u8((A + src2[x] + 64) >> 7)
+ *
+ * WHY THE ARITHMETIC IS EXACT
+ *   - Every epel filter has the sign pattern (-, +, +, -), e.g.
+ *     {-2, 58, 10, -2}. So F on 8-bit samples is
+ *     vmull.u8(s1, f1) + vmlal.u8(s2, f2) - vmlsl.u8(s0, |f0|) - vmlsl.u8(s3, |f3|):
+ *     unsigned 8 x 8 multiplies. The true sum lies within +-(255 * 84), so
+ *     it fits int16; the unsigned 16-bit arithmetic wraps to exactly those
+ *     bits, which are then read as signed.
+ *   - hv's second pass is on int16 values: 16 x 16 -> 32-bit
+ *     multiply-accumulates (vmull.s16 / vmlal.s16), then ">> 6" and
+ *     narrowing (vshrn keeps bits 6..21; the value fits int16).
+ *   - Rounding and clipping: vqrshrun.s16 #n computes (x + 2^(n-1)) >> n
+ *     without overflow and saturates to 0..255: exactly clip_u8((x + 32) >> 6)
+ *     for n = 6, and ... + 64) >> 7 for n = 7.
+ *   - Kind 2 adds src2 with vqadd.s16 (saturating). Where the true sum
+ *     doesn't fit 16 bits the C's result is clipped anyway: above 32767 it
+ *     is 255, and so is the saturated 32767's; below -32768 both give 0.
+ *
+ * HOW
+ *   Columns in strips of 8. For each strip, its rows in turn: the vertical
+ *   filter keeps the 4 rows it needs in registers and loads one new row a
+ *   step. The last strip of a width of 2, 4 or 6 (widths are always even)
+ *   computes 8 but stores only those; nothing outside the block is
+ *   written. Loads of the last strip may read up to 8 bytes past the
+ *   block's right edge (inside the reference picture's padding, as the
+ *   x86 versions do).
+ *
+ * RISC OS
+ *   Byte-element loads and stores (vld1.8 / vst1.8, whole registers or
+ *   one byte lane) with no alignment qualifier, which never fault whatever
+ *   the address: RISC OS runs with alignment checking on, and wider
+ *   elements fault on addresses that aren't a multiple of their size
+ *   (docs/NEON.md). The one exception says so in the instruction: the
+ *   int16 tail's 16-bit lane stores carry ":16", because an int16_t
+ *   destination is always 2-byte aligned.
+ *   tools/neon-align.py --check finds nothing to change here; run.sh
+ *   keeps it that way.
+ *
+ * The C side, hevcdsp_init_neon.c: wrappers that pick the filter
+ * (ff_hevc_epel_filters[mx - 1] / [my - 1]), pass the width, and for hv
+ * run the horizontal pass into a stack buffer then the vertical one.
+ *
+ * Written for riscos-ffmpeg (patch 0016).
+ *
+ * This file is part of FFmpeg.
